WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.394
Stage eight: fuel stop.

2
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:14.278
Camille types /log, then one sentence: two gels and half a litre, left knee three out of ten, RPE seven.

3
00:00:14.378 --> 00:00:17.148
The model spots five pieces.

4
00:00:17.998 --> 00:00:23.192
But it computes nothing: it hands these entities to a small Python script.

5
00:00:23.292 --> 00:00:30.406
The script finds each product in the catalogue: two gels, twenty-five grams each, fifty grams.

6
00:00:30.506 --> 00:00:35.052
The model extracts, the script computes. Never the reverse.

7
00:00:35.902 --> 00:00:38.960
An unknown product? Two similar bars?

8
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.806
The coach asks. It never invents a value, and the rest of the sentence doesn't wait.

9
00:00:45.656 --> 00:00:49.290
Carbs, fluid and effort join the session file.

10
00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.704
The pain goes to the health folder.

11
00:00:51.804 --> 00:00:55.006
Nothing is overwritten or counted twice.

12
00:00:55.856 --> 00:00:59.346
Three out of ten: logged, nothing more.

13
00:00:59.446 --> 00:01:07.712
At seven out of ten, an adjustable threshold, it recommends seeing a professional. Not a substitute for medical advice.

14
00:01:08.562 --> 00:01:14.956
On runs over ninety minutes, the script keeps your best real rate: fifty-six grams an hour.

15
00:01:15.056 --> 00:01:22.434
Ten more for margin: a ceiling of sixty-six. Weigh-ins before and after also give the sweat rate.

16
00:01:23.284 --> 00:01:27.686
Race day: sixty grams of carbs an hour, under the ceiling.

17
00:01:27.786 --> 00:01:37.156
After each run, one line sets Garmin's energy figure against the model's: flagged beyond fifteen percent. Garmin stays the reference.

18
00:01:39.706 --> 00:01:43.484
To go further: the “log” page of the documentation.
